NASA HUNCH Raspberry Pi Server Farm

This project has two main parts:
1. `Cluster/`: monitoring and maintenance stack (Prometheus, Grafana, backend, exporters).
2. `deployer/`: generic app hosting deployer for queued app contracts.

The deployer supports:
1. Standard web/backend container apps.
2. AI apps (scheduled onto AI-labeled nodes).
3. Apps built from a Git repo containing a Dockerfile.

Quick hardware plan used by this repo:
1. `2 x Raspberry Pi 5 (8GB)` -> `role=ai`
2. `3 x Raspberry Pi 5 (4GB)` -> `role=general`
3. Use one 8GB Pi as the Swarm manager.

Quick note on where the code should live:
1. Clone the repo on the Swarm manager Pi (required).
2. The deployer and stack commands run from the manager Pi.
3. Docker images must be available on nodes that run them (use a registry or pre-build on nodes if needed).

Pre-Presentation Setup (all Pis)
1. Install Raspberry Pi OS 64-bit.
2. Connect all Pis to the same network.
3. Install Docker:
   `curl -fsSL https://get.docker.com | sh`
4. Add your user to Docker group and reload shell:
   `sudo usermod -aG docker $USER`
   `newgrp docker`
5. Install tools:
   `sudo apt update && sudo apt upgrade -y`
   `sudo apt install -y python3 python3-pip git`

Swarm Setup (manager Pi)
1. Initialize Swarm:
   `docker swarm init`
2. Get worker join command:
   `docker swarm join-token worker`
3. Run the printed join command on each worker Pi.
4. Verify nodes:
   `docker node ls`

Node Labels (for 2 x 8GB + 3 x 4GB)
1. Label 8GB Pis as AI:
   `docker node update --label-add role=ai <8gb-pi-1>`
   `docker node update --label-add role=ai <8gb-pi-2>`
2. Label 4GB Pis as general:
   `docker node update --label-add role=general <4gb-pi-1>`
   `docker node update --label-add role=general <4gb-pi-2>`
   `docker node update --label-add role=general <4gb-pi-3>`
3. Verify labels:
   `docker node inspect <node-name> --format '{{json .Spec.Labels}}'`

Manager Pi Repo Setup
1. Clone repo:
   `cd ~ && git clone <your-repo-url> NasaHunch`
2. Install deployer/test dependencies (minimal):
   `cd ~/NasaHunch`
   `pip3 install --user pyyaml pytest`

Build Monitoring Stack Images (manager Pi)
Run from `~/NasaHunch/Cluster`:
1. `docker build -t cluster_sensor_exporter ./simulated_nodes`
2. `docker build -t cluster_backup_service ./backup_service`
3. `docker build -t cluster_disk_health ./disk_health`
4. `docker build -t cluster_manager ./cluster_manager`
5. `docker build -t cluster_test_stress ./test_stress`
6. `docker build -t cluster_log_aggregator ./log_aggregator`
7. `docker build -t cluster_backend ./backend`

Deploy Monitoring Stack
1. From `~/NasaHunch/Cluster`:
   `docker stack deploy -c docker-stack.yml cluster`
2. Verify:
   `docker stack services cluster`
   `docker service ls`

Quick Smoke Checks (do before audience arrives)
1. `docker node ls`
2. `docker stack services cluster`
3. `docker service ps cluster_sensor_exporter`
4. `docker service logs cluster_sensor_exporter --tail 20`
5. `curl http://localhost:9200/metrics | head`
6. `curl http://localhost:9090/-/healthy`

Presentation Runbook (fast flow)
1. Show cluster + node roles:
   `docker node ls`
   `docker node inspect <8gb-pi-1> --format '{{json .Spec.Labels}}'`
   `docker node inspect <4gb-pi-1> --format '{{json .Spec.Labels}}'`
2. Show monitoring stack:
   `cd ~/NasaHunch/Cluster`
   `docker stack services cluster`
   `docker service logs cluster_sensor_exporter --tail 20`
   `docker service logs cluster_prometheus --tail 20`
3. Show live metrics in browser (replace `<manager-ip>`):
   `http://<manager-ip>:9200/metrics`
   `http://<manager-ip>:9090/targets`
   `http://<manager-ip>:9090/graph` (query `sensor_temperature_c`, `sensor_humidity_pct`)
   `http://<manager-ip>:3000` (Grafana)
4. Show hosting functionality (safe first):
   `cd ~/NasaHunch`
   `python3 deployer/deployer.py --queue-dir queue --dry-run`
5. Show real hosting deployment:
   `python3 deployer/deployer.py --queue-dir queue`
6. Verify hosted services + scheduling:
   `docker service ls`
   `docker service ps test-web`
   `docker service ps test-ai-ollama`
   `docker service ps test-git-app`

What to point out during hosting demo
1. `sample_web.yaml` schedules to `role=general`.
2. `sample_ai.yaml` schedules to `role=ai`.
3. `sample_git_app.yaml` can build from a Git repo (dry-run is safest live demo for this one).
4. Check `NODE` column in `docker service ps` to prove placement.

Useful Logs / Debug Commands During Presentation
1. `docker stack services cluster`
2. `docker service ls`
3. `docker service ps cluster_sensor_exporter`
4. `docker service ps cluster_prometheus`
5. `docker service logs cluster_sensor_exporter --tail 50`
6. `docker service logs cluster_prometheus --tail 50`
7. `docker service logs cluster_backend --tail 50`
8. `docker service logs test-web --tail 50`
9. `docker service logs test-ai-ollama --tail 50`

Queue examples
1. `queue/sample_web.yaml` (web app image)
2. `queue/sample_ai.yaml` (AI app image, `role=ai` placement)
3. `queue/sample_git_app.yaml` (build image from Git repo)

Run unit tests (manager Pi, optional)
1. `pytest -v deployer/test_deployer.py`

Known Demo Caveats (current repo)
1. `Cluster/prometheus/prometheus.yml` references `node_exporter:9100`, but `node_exporter` is not defined in `Cluster/docker-stack.yml` (Prometheus may show one target down).
2. `Cluster/backend/backend/urls.py` currently exposes `/admin/` only; monitor API routes are not wired into main URLs.
3. `sample_git_app.yaml` real deployment may fail on worker nodes if the built image exists only on the manager and no registry is used.
4. Safer live demo: real deploy `sample_web` + `sample_ai`, use `sample_git_app` in `--dry-run`.

Cleanup After Demo
1. `docker stack rm cluster`
2. `docker service rm test-web test-ai-ollama test-git-app`
